 SEAM 4: the whole ride, from the start line to the finish, checked by INVARIANTS.

 THIS IS THE ONE SEAM NOTES NAMED AND NOTHING CLOSED. Every other check in judge/
 grades ONE STEP FROM A SHARED STATE, and that is not an accident: NOTES section 4
 argues that `rider.decide` picks the lean by BINARY SEARCH over twelve float
 comparisons, so a 1e-7 f64-against-f32 difference will eventually flip one and the
 two rides are on different trajectories from there. A long trace comparison would
 go red and it would NOT mean the port is wrong. What it prescribes instead is this:
 "For a whole-ride check, check INVARIANTS (on the road, no loop, sunset monotone)
 -- which is what games/driving/test/test_model.ts already does for the TS."

 SO NEITHER ARM LOOKS AT THE OTHER'S TRAJECTORY. The probe runs the GAME's ride and
 asserts the list on every frame; this runs the PORT's own ride and asserts the same
 list; the two compare only where each invariant FIRST failed, or -1 for never.
 -1 against -1 is a statement about two rides that were never required to be the
 same ride, which is exactly the strength NOTES said was available and no more.

 AND THE DRIFT ITSELF IS NOW A NUMBER. NOTES predicted it and nothing had ever
 measured it. Both arms sample ROUTE DISTANCE every hundredth frame -- the one
 quantity that rises monotonically for a whole ride, so a gap in it is the gap
 between the two rides, in metres -- and the last line below reports the first
 sample where they part by a centimetre. It is a MEASUREMENT and not a gate,
 because NOTES' whole argument is that this number is allowed to be finite.

 WHAT IS DRIFT-IMMUNE AND WHAT IS NOT, stated rather than assumed. The eight
 verdicts are. So is the ORDER of segments visited: a route is a route whatever the
 last bit does, and a ride that skipped one, repeated one or took them out of order
 is broken in a way no epsilon excuses. The FRAME COUNT is not drift-immune -- a
 flipped lean changes a corner speed and the finish arrives a frame or two later --
 so it is measured and reported, not graded. What it is graded against is the CAP:
 a ride that has not finished by 20,000 frames has not terminated, and `rd-finished`
 is where "no loop" turns into a number.

 THE INVARIANT LIST IS test_model.ts's `assertInvariants`, ported bound for bound
 and epsilon for epsilon, including the entry floor. A rider may sit BEFORE a
 segment's start -- he crosses into a turn at the inner edge, just shy of the begin
 line -- and the floor is -width/sin(entry angle) because the worst case is crossing
 at the OUTER road edge rather than the centre. `exit-to` is [1, 2, ..., 18, 18], so
 segment i is entered from i-1 and segment 0 has no entry intersection.

 FINITENESS COMES FREE FROM THE BOUNDS, as long as they are written as things that
 must HOLD rather than things that must not be exceeded. Every comparison against a
 NaN is false, so `v >= -1e-9 and v <= 8` fails on a NaN v and the frame is recorded.
 Writing it the other way round -- flagging only when a bound is exceeded -- would
 let a NaN through every gate here.

 ABSOLUTE VALUE COMES FROM `Judge chapter Grade`, which this already cites. The
 first draft defined its own `ck-abs`, three lines identical to `g-abs` sitting in
 a chapter already in scope. Grade duplicates DeviceMath's `real-abs` ON PURPOSE --
 a judge must not be built out of what it judges -- and that argument does not
 extend to a second copy inside the judge.

 We say:

Section: The Invariants

 test_model.ts's numbers. QUARTER is a right angle; the three epsilons are its own
 and they differ from each other on purpose -- the gaze is a view angle assembled
 from more parts than the bike's yaw, so it is allowed a thousand times the slack.

  ride-cap : Integer
  ride-cap = 20000

  yaw-slack : Real
  yaw-slack = 0.000001

  gaze-slack : Real
  gaze-slack = 0.001

  along-slack : Real
  along-slack = 0.000001

  v-floor : Real
  v-floor = 0.0 - 0.000000001

  v-ceiling : Real
  v-ceiling = 8.0

  across-slack : Real
  across-slack = 1.0

 A centimetre over a seven-kilometre route: far above the f32/f64 floor of about
 1.2e-7 relative, and far below anything a rider could be said to be somewhere
 else. Sampling every hundredth frame is enough to place the parting within a
 second and a half of riding.

  sample-every : Integer
  sample-every = 100

  part-metres : Real
  part-metres = 0.01

  part-metres-mm : Integer
  part-metres-mm = 10

  entry-floor : List Segment, Integer -> Real
  entry-floor (w) (i) =
    if i <= 0 then 0.0
    else let a = (list-at w (i - 1)).exit-angle
      in if a > 0.0 then 0.0 - (list-at w i).width / r-sin a else 0.0

Section: The Fold

 ONE RECORD CARRIED DOWN THE RIDE. Eight first-failure frames rather than a list of
 eight, because a list would want updating at an index and Codex has no such thing;
 eight fields say which invariant in their names, which a list index would not.

  RideCk = record {
    frame : Integer,
    bad-v : Integer,
    bad-yaw : Integer,
    bad-floor : Integer,
    bad-ceil : Integer,
    bad-across : Integer,
    bad-gaze : Integer,
    bad-focus : Integer,
    bad-dusk : Integer,
    segs : List Integer,
    enters : List Integer,
    sample : List Real,
    dusk : Real,
    finished : Boolean
  }

 THE FIRST failure is what is kept, so a ride that goes wrong once reports the frame
 it went wrong rather than the last frame it was still wrong on.

  keep : Integer, Boolean, Integer -> Integer
  keep (cur) (ok) (frame) = if ok then cur else if cur >= 0 then cur else frame

 A segment is recorded when it CHANGES, so the result is the route as travelled --
 nineteen entries for a ride that visits each once in order, and something else for
 a ride that does not.

  seen-segs : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  seen-segs (segs) (i) =
    if list-length segs <= 0 then [i]
    else if list-at segs (list-length segs - 1) == i then segs
    else segs & [i]

  ride-fold : List Segment, RiderState, RideCk -> RideCk
  ride-fold (w) (s) (c) =
    let seg = list-at w s.segment
    in let d = sun-set-fraction (real-from-int c.frame)
    in let c2 = RideCk {
         frame = c.frame,
         bad-v = keep c.bad-v (s.v >= v-floor and s.v <= v-ceiling) c.frame,
         bad-yaw = keep c.bad-yaw (g-abs s.yaw <= half-pi + yaw-slack) c.frame,
         bad-floor = keep c.bad-floor (s.along >= entry-floor w s.segment - along-slack) c.frame,
         bad-ceil = keep c.bad-ceil (s.along <= seg.length + along-slack) c.frame,
         bad-across = keep c.bad-across (g-abs s.across <= seg.width / 2.0 + across-slack) c.frame,
         bad-gaze = keep c.bad-gaze (g-abs s.gaze-yaw <= half-pi + gaze-slack) c.frame,
         bad-focus = keep c.bad-focus (s.focus >= 0.0 and s.focus <= 1.0) c.frame,
         bad-dusk = keep c.bad-dusk (d >= c.dusk) c.frame,
         segs = seen-segs c.segs s.segment,
         enters = if list-length c.segs > 0 and list-at c.segs (list-length c.segs - 1) == s.segment
                    then c.enters else c.enters & [c.frame],
         sample = if c.frame - c.frame / sample-every * sample-every == 0
                    then c.sample & [route-distance w s.segment s.along] else c.sample,
         dusk = d,
         finished = c.finished }
    in if is-finished s w then RideCk {
         frame = c2.frame, bad-v = c2.bad-v, bad-yaw = c2.bad-yaw,
         bad-floor = c2.bad-floor, bad-ceil = c2.bad-ceil, bad-across = c2.bad-across,
         bad-gaze = c2.bad-gaze, bad-focus = c2.bad-focus, bad-dusk = c2.bad-dusk,
         segs = c2.segs, enters = c2.enters, sample = c2.sample, dusk = c2.dusk, finished = True }
       else if c2.frame >= ride-cap then c2
       else ride-fold w (get-next-rider-state s w) (RideCk {
         frame = c2.frame + 1, bad-v = c2.bad-v, bad-yaw = c2.bad-yaw,
         bad-floor = c2.bad-floor, bad-ceil = c2.bad-ceil, bad-across = c2.bad-across,
         bad-gaze = c2.bad-gaze, bad-focus = c2.bad-focus, bad-dusk = c2.bad-dusk,
         segs = c2.segs, enters = c2.enters, sample = c2.sample, dusk = c2.dusk, finished = c2.finished })

 The dusk seed is below every value sun-set-fraction can take, so frame 0 cannot
 fail the monotone test against a value that was never computed.

  ride-start : RideCk
  ride-start = RideCk {
    frame = 0, bad-v = 0 - 1, bad-yaw = 0 - 1, bad-floor = 0 - 1, bad-ceil = 0 - 1,
    bad-across = 0 - 1, bad-gaze = 0 - 1, bad-focus = 0 - 1, bad-dusk = 0 - 1,
    segs = [], enters = [], sample = [], dusk = 0.0 - 1.0, finished = False }

  ride-result : RideCk
  ride-result = ride-fold build-world initial-rider-state ride-start

 EVERY LINE BELOW READS THE RIDE THROUGH ONE `let`, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.
 `ride-result` is a nullary binding, and a nullary binding is RE-EVALUATED at every
 use -- there is no memoisation, so each mention of it runs the whole 6,960-frame
 fold again. Written the obvious way, with `ride-result.frame` here and
 `ride-result.segs` there, this check ran the ride THIRTEEN times, took twelve
 seconds, and then exhausted the 4 GiB bump heap on the last line, because nothing
 is ever reclaimed (PORTING_NOTES C6) and thirteen rides do not fit where one does.
 Binding it once and passing it down costs a `let` and turns thirteen rides into
 one. PORTING_NOTES B16.

  first-bads : RideCk -> List Integer
  first-bads (r) = [r.bad-v, r.bad-yaw, r.bad-floor, r.bad-ceil, r.bad-across,
                    r.bad-gaze, r.bad-focus, r.bad-dusk]

 THE FIRST SAMPLE AT WHICH THE TWO RIDES ARE A CENTIMETRE APART, over whichever of
 the two is shorter -- the port finishes at its own frame, so the two sample lists
 need not be the same length and the shorter one is the honest overlap.

  shorter : RideCk -> Integer
  shorter (r) = if list-length r.sample < list-length g-rd-sample
                  then list-length r.sample else list-length g-rd-sample

  part-at : RideCk, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  part-at (r) (n) (i) =
    if i >= n then 0 - 1
    else if g-abs (list-at r.sample i - list-at g-rd-sample i) > part-metres then i
    else part-at r n (i + 1)

 The gap at the LAST sample both rides reached, in millimetres, because `show` on a
 Real is still refused.

  final-gap-mm : RideCk, Integer -> Integer
  final-gap-mm (r) (n) =
    real-to-int (g-abs (list-at r.sample (n - 1) - list-at g-rd-sample (n - 1)) * 1000.0)

Section: Entry

 THE FRAME COUNT AND THE PARTING ARE PRINTED, NOT GRADED, and each line says so
 where it is read.

 WHICH SEGMENT THE TWO RIDES DISAGREE ABOUT MOST, in frames. Not a gate and not
 drift-immune -- it is the line that localises the drift, and localising it is what
 turned "the counts differ by forty" into an answer. Most segments match to a frame
 or two; a few do not, and probe_sens.zig shows the GAME doing exactly the same to
 itself under a persistent bias of 1e-7, which is f32's own resolution.

  seg-span : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  seg-span (es) (i) = list-at es (i + 1) - list-at es i

  worst-seg : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  worst-seg (es) (n) (i) (best) (bestd) =
    if i + 1 >= n then best
    else let d = seg-span es i - seg-span g-rd-enters i
      in let a = if d < 0 then 0 - d else d
      in if a > bestd then worst-seg es n (i + 1) i a else worst-seg es n (i + 1) best bestd

  seg-overlap : RideCk -> Integer
  seg-overlap (r) = if list-length r.enters < list-length g-rd-enters
                      then list-length r.enters else list-length g-rd-enters

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let r = ride-result
    in let n = shorter r
    in let p = part-at r n 0
    in act
      print-line-uni (grade-ints  "rd-firstbad " (first-bads r) g-rd-firstbad)
      print-line-uni (grade-ints  "rd-segs     " r.segs g-rd-segs)
      print-line-uni (grade-bools "rd-finished " [r.finished] g-rd-finished)
      print-line-uni ("rd-frames   port " & show r.frame & " zig " & show (list-at g-rd-frames 0) & " (reported, not graded: a flipped lean moves it)")
      print-line-uni ("rd-worst    segment " & show (worst-seg r.enters (seg-overlap r) 0 0 0) & " is where they differ most: " & show (seg-span r.enters (worst-seg r.enters (seg-overlap r) 0 0 0)) & " frames against " & show (seg-span g-rd-enters (worst-seg r.enters (seg-overlap r) 0 0 0)) & " (reported, not graded)")
      print-line-uni ("rd-part     the rides reach 10 mm apart at sample " & show p & " of " & show n & ", frame " & show (p * sample-every) & "; " & show (final-gap-mm r n) & " mm apart at the last shared sample")
    end
